/*
:name: implicit_clock_two_explicit
:type: equiv
:verilog_top: implicit_clock_two_explicit
:pyrope_top: implicit_clock_two_explicit
*/
// User ruling 2026-09-27 (04b "Implicit clock and reset"): two implicit-clock
// candidates (`clock` and `clk`) are a compile error only "when a register
// relies on the implicit one" (errors/implicit_clock_ambiguous.prp). A
// multi-clock module that names the clock on EVERY register is legal and must
// keep compiling to two separate clock domains: a guard against an over-eager
// fix that errors whenever two candidate inputs exist. Both registers use the
// single implicit reset `rst`.
//
// prp-equiv and prp-statematch pass today. prp-v2prp2v FAILS today ("native
// Pyrope proof" REFUTED): the slang reader (lower_ff_process, `implicit_clk`)
// omits `clock_pin` for ANY process clocked by an input named `clk` or
// `clock`, so the golden reads back as two implicit-clock registers and tolg
// binds both to the first candidate, `clock`. The same collapse happens to
// BOTH sides of `lhd lec` (it reads Verilog with slang), which is why native
// lec also proves a golden with the two clocks SWAPPED; only the lgyosys
// cross-check refutes that one. Once ruling 10 lands in tolg, the reader must
// write an explicit `clock_pin=` whenever the module has both inputs, or
// every Verilog read of this golden becomes the ambiguous-clock error.
// Since ruling 52 (2026-09-29) clocks bind by TYPE: both inputs are `Clock`,
// so the module has no implicit clock and each register names its own; the
// single `Reset` input `rst` stays the implicit reset.
pub mod implicit_clock_two_explicit(clock:Clock, clk:Clock, rst:Reset, d:U4) -> (q:U4@[1], p:U5@[1]) {
  reg r:U4:[clock_pin=clk] = 5
  reg s:U5:[clock_pin=clock] = 9
  r = d
  s = d + 1
  q = r
  p = s
}
